{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1521469945,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUzMDY0ODc2OTE=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2322","origin":"github"},"title":"Singularity image limitation","message":"if you run: `datalad run \u003cscript\u003e` with `datalad` being the singularity container, you can run into some unintuitive situation where it will fail with `FileNotFound`. The reason could be that the script has a shebang with an interpreter that is not available in the image (e.g. `python` instead of `python3`).\n\nIn general this is probably the most severe limitation of the singularity approach. `run` executes any command inside the container, so everything has to be in the container. If the container is tuned towards a particular type of analysis this would be exactly what you want, but in case of a generic datalad installation in a container, this is a bummer.","files":null}]}